  Any hostname not in the dev set falls back to [1mproduction[0m. That's deliberate: an unrecognized host is far more likely
  to be a real deployment than a dev box, and silently pointing a real user's login at a dev API is the worse failure.
  The tradeoff is that a typo'd dev hostname fails toward production instead of erroring loudly.

  [3mAssumption: [38;5;153mhttps://api.dev.example.com[39m is a placeholder — validate by replacing it with your real dev host before [0m
  [3mthis ships.[0m

  [1mFiles touched[0m

  - [1mnew[0m [38;5;153msettings.js[39m
  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — drop [38;5;153mconst API_ENDPOINT[39m, add [38;5;153mimport { settings } from "./settings.js"[39m, point the stub comment at
    [38;5;153msettings.endpoints.login[39m
  - [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js"></script>[39m
  - [38;5;153msrc/[39m — [1muntouched[0m

  After this, viewing the page needs a local server ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m) rather than opening the file directly — the
  cost of native modules we discussed.

  [1mTooling[0m

  The repo has no linter, no test runner, and no dependencies, so there's no established testing pattern for me to
  follow. This change is the cheapest moment to add one if you want it — [38;5;153mresolveEnvironmentName[39m is a pure function and
  would be a natural first test.

